llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
132): on gfx803, the xor butterflies in ggml's warp reductions, the MoE
top-k and the q8_1 quantizers take their cross-lane steps from DPP row
rotations and quad permutations instead of ds_bpermute, with one swizzle
for the step that crosses DPP rows. The results are the same bits.
gfx803-specific: the new path is compiled only for GCN4 (__gfx803__).

WHY
---
HIP turns __shfl_xor_sync into ds_bpermute_b32 on GCN. Each one computes a
byte address, goes out to the LDS unit and back, and the next step of a
reduction waits for it, so a 32-lane reduction is five such round trips in
a row. Single-token decode is made of small kernels whose time is mostly
this kind of latency. The MoE top-k of Qwen3.6 35B-A3B picks 8 of 256
experts in 8 rounds of a 5-step argmax: 15 us a call, 40 calls a token, on
the path from the router to the CPU experts. rms_norm, the q8_1 quantizer,
the mmvq epilogues and the DeltaNet recurrence reduce the same way.

DPP reads another lane's register inside a VALU instruction. gfx803's DPP
rotates within a row of 16 lanes and permutes within a quad; it has no xor
pattern beyond the quad. In a butterfly that runs from the widest offset
down, the wider steps have already made each lane equal to every lane whose
index differs from its own only in higher bits, so at offsets 8, 4, 2 and 1
the lane that a rotation of the row reaches holds exactly the value of the
xor partner. Each step combines the same two values in the same order as
before, and every lane ends with the same bits. Offset 16 crosses rows and
uses ds_swizzle, which needs no address register; offset 32 (a reduction
over the whole 64-lane wave) stays a shuffle.
Butterflies over groups narrower than 16 lanes take the swizzle at offset
4, where no rotation lines up.

WHAT
----
common.cuh: ggml_cuda_butterfly_xor<group>(x, offset) for int, float and
half2, used by warp_reduce_sum, warp_reduce_max, warp_reduce_all and
warp_reduce_any. topk-moe.cu and quantize.cu use it in their own
butterflies. The Hadamard transform in fwht keeps its shuffles: its
partners hold different values, so no rotation can stand in for them.

Result on an RX 590 (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, experts in system RAM, q8_0
KV cache, flash attention):

    logits, llama-perplexity over 1,024 tokens as one-token decodes
    and as 512-token batches; Qwen3 8B decodes; Qwen3.5 9B decodes
    with 134 as well                                               byte-identical
    GPU kernel time per token (rocprof, trimmed means)             11.13 -> 10.81 ms
      topk_moe                                                     14.9 -> 12.2 us
    ds_bpermute_b32 in libggml-hip.so                              50,474 -> 5,798
    llama-bench tg256, four interleaved pairs (tok/s)
      without   26.43  26.49  26.69  26.82
      with      27.09  26.89  26.95  28.08

Verified on an RX 590: test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 passes all 16,460
cases with this patch and 134.

To reproduce, build llama.cpp b11232 with GGML_HIP=ON and
AMDGPU_TARGETS=gfx803, then with and without the patch:

    llama-perplexity -m <model> -ngl 99 -ncmoe 99 -fa on -ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0
        -f <text> -c 512 --chunks 2 -b 512 -ub 1 --kl-divergence-base <file>
        (GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1; the two files compare equal)
    llama-bench -m <model> -ngl 99 -ncmoe 99 -lm none -fa 1 -ctk q8_0
        -ctv q8_0 -p 0 -n 256

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh
@@ -451,6 +451,54 @@
     }
 };
 
+// One step of an xor butterfly over groups of `group` lanes that runs from offset group/2 down to 1.
+// On GCN __shfl_xor becomes ds_bpermute, which goes through the LDS unit and puts its latency on every
+// step of a reduction. When a step with offset 8 or less runs, the wider steps before it have given every
+// lane the same value as the lanes whose index differs from it only in higher bits, so the lane `offset`
+// places along a rotation of its 16-lane DPP row holds exactly what the xor partner holds, and the VALU
+// reads it with a row rotation: the result has the same bits as the shuffle's. Offset 16 crosses DPP rows
+// and is a swizzle within 32 lanes, which needs no address register; offset 32 stays a shuffle.
+template <int group>
+static __device__ __forceinline__ int ggml_cuda_butterfly_xor(int x, int offset) {
+#if defined(GGML_USE_HIP) && defined(GCN4)
+    if (offset == 16) {
+        return __builtin_amdgcn_ds_swizzle(x, 0x401F);
+    }
+    if (offset == 8) {
+        return __builtin_amdgcn_mov_dpp(x, 0x128, 0xF, 0xF, false); // row_ror:8
+    }
+    if (offset == 4) {
+        return group >= 16 ? __builtin_amdgcn_mov_dpp(x, 0x124, 0xF, 0xF, false) // row_ror:4
+                           : __builtin_amdgcn_ds_swizzle(x, 0x101F);
+    }
+    if (offset == 2) {
+        return __builtin_amdgcn_mov_dpp(x, 0x4E, 0xF, 0xF, false); // quad_perm:[2,3,0,1]
+    }
+    if (offset == 1) {
+        return __builtin_amdgcn_mov_dpp(x, 0xB1, 0xF, 0xF, false); // quad_perm:[1,0,3,2]
+    }
+#endif // defined(GGML_USE_HIP) && defined(GCN4)
+    return __shfl_xor_sync(0xffffffff, x, offset, group);
+}
+
+template <int group>
+static __device__ __forceinline__ float ggml_cuda_butterfly_xor(float x, int offset) {
+#if defined(GGML_USE_HIP) && defined(GCN4)
+    return __int_as_float(ggml_cuda_butterfly_xor<group>(__float_as_int(x), offset));
+#else
+    return __shfl_xor_sync(0xffffffff, x, offset, group);
+#endif // defined(GGML_USE_HIP) && defined(GCN4)
+}
+
+template <int group>
+static __device__ __forceinline__ half2 ggml_cuda_butterfly_xor(half2 x, int offset) {
+#if defined(GGML_USE_HIP) && defined(GCN4)
+    return __builtin_bit_cast(half2, ggml_cuda_butterfly_xor<group>(__builtin_bit_cast(int, x), offset));
+#else
+    return __shfl_xor_sync(0xffffffff, x, offset, group);
+#endif // defined(GGML_USE_HIP) && defined(GCN4)
+}
+
